WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.448 --> 00:00:08.330
Hello again!

2
00:00:15.374 --> 00:00:16.114
Faye Moss.

3
00:00:16.875 --> 00:00:23.979
Welcome to Superstars in History, the talk show that brings you exclusive interviews with famous people from the

4
00:00:24.099 --> 00:00:32.424
past. Today's guest was not only a famous scientist but also an advocate for peace and equality.

5
00:00:33.044 --> 00:00:41.510
Please welcome the genius who helped us understand the universe, Albert Einstein!

6
00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:55.881
Hello, Faye!

7
00:00:57.282 --> 00:00:59.204
I brought you an autographed photo.

8
00:01:04.097 --> 00:01:06.578
This picture is hilarious!

9
00:01:06.778 --> 00:01:07.558
Thank you!

10
00:01:07.578 --> 00:01:10.699
Were you always so funny?

11
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.081
No, I don't think so.

12
00:01:15.621 --> 00:01:19.023
In fact, as a child I was rather quiet and serious.

13
00:01:19.963 --> 00:01:25.926
I was born in 1879 in the German town of Ulm and grew up in Munich.

14
00:01:26.806 --> 00:01:31.608
At an early age, maybe four or five, my father showed me a compass.

15
00:01:32.352 --> 00:01:38.256
I was amazed by it and wondered why the needle always pointed in one direction.

16
00:01:39.397 --> 00:01:41.999
This began my interest in science.

17
00:01:44.521 --> 00:01:46.742
I was also very good in mathematics.

18
00:01:48.383 --> 00:01:50.164
I guess you enjoyed school!

19
00:01:51.966 --> 00:01:53.667
Then you guess wrong, Faye.

20
00:01:54.287 --> 00:01:56.449
I didn't enjoy school much at all.

21
00:01:57.129 --> 00:02:01.012
My family was Jewish, but most people in Munich were Catholic.

22
00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:04.102
I was bullied because I was different.

23
00:02:05.964 --> 00:02:11.248
I also didn't make a great impression on many of my teachers since I preferred to learn things

24
00:02:11.348 --> 00:02:14.951
my own way—and often knew more than my teachers!

25
00:02:16.553 --> 00:02:21.857
When I was a teenager, my parents moved to Italy, and I stayed behind in Munich.

26
00:02:22.498 --> 00:02:27.122
I was supposed to finish my education, but I soon left Germany too.

27
00:02:28.323 --> 00:02:29.104
Where did you go?

28
00:02:30.396 --> 00:02:34.599
I joined my family in Italy and later finished my schooling in Switzerland.

29
00:02:35.159 --> 00:02:39.541
I also gave up my German citizenship to avoid being drafted into the military.

30
00:02:39.962 --> 00:02:45.065
I didn't do well with organized structure in school, so I certainly wouldn't have done well in the

31
00:02:45.105 --> 00:02:49.927
military! Eventually I attended the Polytechnic School in Zurich.

32
00:02:50.428 --> 00:02:58.889
Once again I struggled with the strict authority of the school system—I skipped many classes—and once again I

33
00:02:59.389 --> 00:03:01.590
knew more than many of my teachers!

34
00:03:02.391 --> 00:03:05.533
But I loved learning and managed to graduate.

35
00:03:06.434 --> 00:03:08.615
What did you do after you finished school?

36
00:03:10.396 --> 00:03:14.379
I had planned to become a teacher, but that was not so easy.

37
00:03:14.959 --> 00:03:21.303
All those skipped classes earned me a reputation for being unreliable, so no one wanted to give me

38
00:03:21.343 --> 00:03:22.104
a teaching job.

39
00:03:23.924 --> 00:03:28.266
Eventually I found work as a patent clerk in Bern, Switzerland.

40
00:03:28.727 --> 00:03:31.708
My job was to review applications for inventions.

41
00:03:32.509 --> 00:03:37.752
It was an easy position that gave me plenty of spare time for my physics experiments.

42
00:03:38.992 --> 00:03:43.255
How were you able to do physics experiments at the patent office?

43
00:03:43.775 --> 00:03:44.995
Didn't you need a lab?

44
00:03:46.276 --> 00:03:53.180
My experiments were Gedankenexperimente—which means thought experiments—so I didn't need a lab!

45
00:03:54.004 --> 00:04:00.767
In my thought experiments, I thought of scientific ways to explain the things I observed.

46
00:04:02.328 --> 00:04:05.890
I came up with some of my best ideas at the patent office.

47
00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:12.633
You were working there in 1905 when you published some groundbreaking scientific papers.

48
00:04:13.553 --> 00:04:19.516
Yes. That year has been called my annus mirabilis—my miracle year.

49
00:04:20.252 --> 00:04:25.616
I published four papers that introduced new ideas that changed the field of physics.

50
00:04:26.457 --> 00:04:33.682
My third paper was on my theory of special relativity, which explains that the relationship between time and

51
00:04:33.802 --> 00:04:35.023
space is relative.

52
00:04:35.583 --> 00:04:39.586
The paper also introduced the idea that the speed of light is a constant.

53
00:04:40.427 --> 00:04:45.170
I expanded on the speed of light in my fourth paper, where I showed that matter and energy

54
00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:47.572
are different forms of the same thing.

55
00:04:48.168 --> 00:04:53.331
This is illustrated by my famous formula: E=mc².

56
00:04:54.852 --> 00:05:00.015
E stands for energy, m for mass, and c is the speed of light.

57
00:05:00.475 --> 00:05:04.357
And this— Whoa—my head is starting to hurt!

58
00:05:05.217 --> 00:05:09.019
Your theories are complicated, but they're certainly useful.

59
00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:15.623
Your work on space, time, and gravity changed our basic ideas about how the universe works.

60
00:05:16.323 --> 00:05:21.113
And, of course, those "miracle year" papers landed you a better job.

61
00:05:22.133 --> 00:05:26.835
Yes, I finally got a teaching position at a university in Switzerland.

62
00:05:27.255 --> 00:05:31.276
I later moved around, teaching at other universities throughout Europe.

63
00:05:31.736 --> 00:05:39.118
Eventually I had a very distinguished professorship at the University of Berlin, but once again I left Germany.

64
00:05:40.078 --> 00:05:46.940
In 1933, while my wife and I were visiting the United States, Hitler came to power in Germany.

65
00:05:47.436 --> 00:05:49.858
Jews were no longer allowed to teach.

66
00:05:51.099 --> 00:05:55.903
Our home was taken by the government, and a bounty was put on my head.

67
00:05:56.764 --> 00:06:02.208
We settled in the U.S., and I began to work at the Institute for Advanced Study at Princeton

68
00:06:02.368 --> 00:06:11.595
University. In 1939 you drafted a letter to American president Franklin Roosevelt warning him that Germany was working

69
00:06:11.675 --> 00:06:12.836
on a nuclear weapon.

70
00:06:14.217 --> 00:06:15.739
You also urged the U.S.

71
00:06:15.759 --> 00:06:17.100
to develop one of its own.

72
00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:18.640
Why did you do that?

73
00:06:19.961 --> 00:06:21.962
I reasoned that if the U.S.

74
00:06:22.202 --> 00:06:26.404
also had a weapon, it might deter Germany from using theirs.

75
00:06:27.064 --> 00:06:32.446
Had I known Germany would not complete theirs, I might not have signed that letter.

76
00:06:33.106 --> 00:06:36.628
As a lifelong pacifist, I regretted my part.

77
00:06:37.088 --> 00:06:41.009
Signing the letter was the one great mistake of my life.

78
00:06:43.751 --> 00:06:48.513
After the war I worked to stop the building of nuclear weapons and promote peace.

79
00:06:49.294 --> 00:06:54.819
I also helped raise money for Jewish causes and worked for racial equality in my new country.

80
00:06:55.459 --> 00:07:01.424
As a Jew, I felt I could understand how African American people felt when they were treated unfairly.

81
00:07:02.806 --> 00:07:04.527
That's so admirable.

82
00:07:05.048 --> 00:07:10.693
We're almost out of time, but before you go, can you tell us the story behind this picture?

83
00:07:12.314 --> 00:07:17.450
It was my 72nd birthday, and I had been smiling for photos for hours.

84
00:07:18.211 --> 00:07:22.735
I was beginning to feel more like a model than a scientist, and I'd had enough!

85
00:07:23.495 --> 00:07:29.601
I stuck out my tongue and quickly turned away, but the photographer was much quicker than I.

86
00:07:30.021 --> 00:07:31.662
The picture turned out well, yes?

87
00:07:32.583 --> 00:07:35.025
I bought copies and sent them to my friends.

88
00:07:35.846 --> 00:07:38.148
I'm glad you consider me a friend!

89
00:07:38.568 --> 00:07:40.610
And thank you so much for the interview.

90
00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:42.872
We'll let you get back to history now.

91
00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:55.543
Good-bye! Throughout his life Albert Einstein earned awards and recognition, including a Nobel Prize.

92
00:07:56.343 --> 00:08:01.485
He continued working at Princeton until his death in 1955 at the age of 76.

93
00:08:01.545 --> 00:08:07.446
Einstein's work completely changed the way we view the universe.

94
00:08:08.427 --> 00:08:15.631
His theories are still in use today and help us position satellites, build nuclear power plants, and more.

95
00:08:16.232 --> 00:08:21.800
He is considered one of the greatest scientists ever—a remarkable genius.

96
00:08:22.741 --> 00:08:24.443
I hope you enjoyed today's guest.

97
00:08:25.024 --> 00:08:30.972
Tune in again next week for another exclusive interview with a famous person from the past.
